course: dictionary
lesson: 3044
title: Meet the earthworm
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An earthworm is a long, soft, pink-brown worm with no legs. It lives in soil and eats dead leaves and bits of plants.
explain: Earthworms do useful work underground. «Earthworms mix soil, make tunnels for air and water, and turn dead leaves into rich soil.» That is why gardeners love them.
explain: Earthworms have no lungs. They breathe through their damp skin, so they must stay moist. That is why you often see them on the surface after heavy rain.
fact: An earthworm is an invertebrate, which means it has no backbone.
fact: «The giant Gippsland earthworm of Australia can grow over a metre long.»
fact: Earthworms' tunnels let air and water into the soil, and their droppings make the soil richer.
fact: Charles Darwin wrote a whole book about worms.
fact: Earthworms breathe through their moist skin, so they need to stay damp.
exercise: «Earthworms eat dead leaves and soil and leave rich casts.» What does an earthworm eat? | *Dead leaves and bits of plants | Sunlight | Metal bits | Fish from the pond
exercise: Why do earthworms often come up after heavy rain? | They want to find a new home in the sky | *They breathe through their skin, which must stay moist | They are hungry for rain
exercise: What is missing from an earthworm's body? | *Legs | Pink-brown colour | Soft skin
test: Which word names a long, soft worm that lives in soil? | Fly | *Earthworm | Snail | Beetle
test: Why do earthworms help gardens? | They drink all the rain | They eat all the flowers | *They make tunnels and turn dead leaves into rich soil | They make the soil hard
exam: Which sentence is true? | Earthworms live in the sea | *Earthworms breathe through damp skin and need to stay moist | Earthworms can fly | Earthworms have lungs and need no water
